@stuartcarnie I had to modify your changes again :cry: 

Your code would not properly deal with this **edge case**:

```cpp
while( true )
{
  rd->bind_uniform_set( us );
  rd->draw();
  memcpy( rd->buffer_persistent_map_advance( dynamic_buffer ), src, size_bytes );
  rd->bind_uniform_set( us );
  rd->draw();
  frame_index = (frame_index + 1) % frame_count;
}
```

What's happening in this case is that `us` gets bound first and then drawn. This uses the dynamic buffers setup in the previous frame.

Then we call buffer_persistent_map_advance() to set them up differently and draw again.

The problem is your Metal driver code would first write to the argument buffer using the first setup, then the second one; which are different but would end up using the same frame_index. So what happens is that either:

1. All commands are sent and flushed, the 1st draw incorrectly uses the argument buffer set for the 2nd draw.
2. If command buffers are sent/flushed earlier, the 2nd draw would overwrite from CPU the argument buffer that could be in use by the GPU processing the 1st draw (i.e. data hazard).

Honestly, if I could, I would ban being able to map the buffer _after_ the UniformSet has already been bound. Most of Godot follows the good pattern (map then bind), but Godot's API offers no guarantee that we could bind using last frame's version then map, then bind again.

It's one of the issues I had while making this PR: Godot is too flexible in this regard. One tiny mistake and we end up binding stale data to graphics (this could happen without UMA and without this PR, I'm ranting about design issues) or causing a data hazard (data hazards would be an UMA thing because CPU writes while GPU reads).

Sending stale data to GPU means the user would see it as some sort of latency problem (because transform data is delayed an extra frame) or see a weird stutter (because some objects are rendered using data from a previous frame, while other objects are using this frame's). I'm constantly keeping an eye on this, because users keep reporting unusually high latency.

I would prefer to use your idea of creating an argument buffer that is `frame_count` bigger, but unfortunately handling this edge case means that we either forbid the edge case (which needs additional code to detect it for validation) or dynamically growing the buffers.

Since you already support different versions of arguments buffers and cache it, I used the same approach I used in D3D12:

Intead of:

```cpp
BoundUniformSet *sus = bound_uniforms.getptr(p_shader);
if (sus != nullptr) {
	sus->merge_into(p_resource_usage);
	return *sus;
}
```

We now do:

```cpp
BoundUniformSet *sus = bound_uniforms.getptr(cache_key);
if (sus != nullptr) {
	sus->merge_into(p_resource_usage);
	return *sus;
}
```

Where `cache_key` is:

```cpp
uint32_t MDUniformSet::calculate_dynamic_state_mask() const {
	uint32_t mask = 0u;
	uint32_t shift = 0u;
	for (const MetalBufferDynamicInfo *dynamic_buffer : dynamic_buffers) {
		DEV_ASSERT(dynamic_buffer->frame_idx < 16u);
		mask |= dynamic_buffer->frame_idx << shift;
		shift += 4u;
	}
	return mask;
}

MDUniformSet::CacheKey cache_key{ p_shader, set.calculate_dynamic_state_mask() };
```

Since we restrict frame\_count to be in range `[2; 4]` and the maximum number of dyn. buffers is 8, a 32-bit mask is enough.

The benefit of this change is that it requires fewer intrusive changes. The disadvantage is that each unique key requires more heap allocations, though this cost should be amortized over time once the cache has been populated.

Because I can't test this code, I ask you if you could test again :face_holding_back_tears:

> The problem is your Metal driver code would first write to the argument buffer using the first setup, then the second one; which are different but would end up using the same frame_index

I'm going to try and explain what is happening in this loop, to see if you agree with my understanding:

```cpp
while( true )
{
  rd->bind_uniform_set( us );
  rd->draw();
  memcpy( rd->buffer_persistent_map_advance( dynamic_buffer ), src, size_bytes );
  rd->bind_uniform_set( us );
  rd->draw();
  frame_index = (frame_index + 1) % frame_count;
}
```

* Assume `dynamic_buffer->frame_idx` is `0`
